  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>(A) Gauss law states that the total electric flux out of a closed surface is equal to the charge enclosed divided by the permittivity. So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>&#8750;</mo><mover><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>&#183;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mover><mi>s</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>q</mi><msub><mo>&#8712;</mo><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></math></p><p>(B) Faraday&rsquo;s law&nbsp;explains that the direction of the current will be opposite to the direction of flux that produced it, so&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8750;</mo><mover><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>&#183;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mover><mi>l</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><msub><mi>&#981;</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>(C) Gauss&#39;s law for magnetism states that the magnetic flux B across any closed surface is zero, so&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8750;</mo><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>&#183;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mover><mi>A</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p>(D) Ampere&rsquo;s Maxwell law is stated as&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8750;</mo><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>&#183;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mover><mi>l</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mo>&#8712;</mo><mn>0</mn></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><msub><mi>&#981;</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>Where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo></math>Conduction current and&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mo>&#8712;</mo><mn>0</mn></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><msub><mi>&#981;</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo></math>Displacement current</p><p>Thus,&nbsp;A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III</p>"
  }
